## 2. Share-alike obligations — these travel with the files

**Two components are licensed CC BY-SA, and share-alike is a condition, not a
courtesy.** If you redistribute these files, or anything derived from them, you
must license that redistribution under CC BY-SA 4.0 (or a compatible licence) and
keep the attribution intact.

| Path | Upstream | Licence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `lexicon/strongs-hebrew.json` | [openscriptures/strongs](https://github.com/openscriptures/strongs) | **CC BY-SA** |
| `lexicon/strongs-greek.json` | [openscriptures/strongs](https://github.com/openscriptures/strongs) | **CC BY-SA** |
| `versions/en/FAMILY 35 NEW TESTAMENT.json` | Wilbur N. Pickering, *The New Testament with Commentary according to Family 35* | **CC BY-SA 4.0** |

Practical consequences:

- **Do not relicense these files** under MIT, Apache-2.0, or any other
  non-share-alike licence.
- **A modified Strong's dictionary is still CC BY-SA.** Reformatting, merging into
  a database, or extracting a subset all produce derivative works.
- **Attribution must survive.** Name the upstream project and licence wherever you
  redistribute.

This does **not** convert the whole repository to CC BY-SA. Share-alike attaches
to those files and to works derived from them, not to independent files merely
distributed alongside them. If you are building a product that mixes this data
with proprietary code, get that boundary reviewed by someone qualified rather
than relying on this paragraph.

### Attribution-only (CC BY) — attribution required, share-alike not

| Path | Upstream | Licence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `lexicon/hebrew.json` | [openscriptures/morphhb](https://github.com/openscriptures/morphhb) (Westminster Leningrad Codex with Strong's lemmas and morphology) | CC BY 4.0 |
| `versions/en/TEXT-CRITICAL ENGLISH NEW TESTAMENT.json` | Robert Adam Boyd | CC BY 4.0 |

### Public domain

| Path | Upstream |
| --- | --- |
| `lexicon/greek.json` | [byztxt/byzantine-majority-text](https://github.com/byztxt/byzantine-majority-text) — Robinson-Pierpont, explicitly placed in the public domain |

`lexicon/SOURCES.md` records the same provenance alongside the build report.

